            "document_hash": "e864eca0a453a831959d2f72710e99833e27d1b0c2f47543510b1a5fb7615fd4",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-e864eca0a453-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Kentucky, the issue before the Court is whether police can impermissibly create exigent circumstances, arising from the improper suppression of reasonably seized evidence after a reasonable warrantless entry."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "e864eca0a453a831959d2f72710e99833e27d1b0c2f47543510b1a5fb7615fd4",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-e864eca0a453-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Ginsburg, does the Kentucky Supreme Court consider the subjective state of mind of police when evaluating warrantless entries?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "e864eca0a453a831959d2f72710e99833e27d1b0c2f47543510b1a5fb7615fd4",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-e864eca0a453-0001-0005"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 30,
              "start": 30
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Kennedy, may police create exigent circumstances and use those circumstances to enter a home?"
      }
    ],
